==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|infatuate|t=foolish|pos=adjective}}, from {{der|en|la|infatuātus}}, past participle of {{m|la|infatuō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{qualifier|verb}} {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈfætjuˌ(w)eɪt/|/ɪnˈfætʃuˌ(w)eɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-infatuate.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{qualifier|adjective|noun}} {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈfætju(w)ət/|/ɪnˈfætʃu(w)ət/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-infatuate2.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[inspire]] with [[unreasoning]] [[love]], [[attachment]] or [[enthusiasm]].
#* {{RQ:Twain Roughing It|page=308|chapter=[https://archive.org/details/cu31924022606465 Chapter 44]|passage=If the mine was a “developed” one, and had no pay ore to show (and of course it hadn’t), we praised the tunnel; said it was one of the most '''infatuating''' tunnels in the land; driveled and driveled about the tunnel till we ran entirely out of ecstasies—but never said a word about the rock.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1888|author=w:George Manville Fenn|title=One Maid’s Mischief|location=New York|publisher=Appleton|chapter=15|page=53|url=https://archive.org/details/onemaidsmischief00fenn
|passage=I declare the girl seems quite to '''infatuate''' the men, and see if trouble does not come of it.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To make [[foolish]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1624|author=w:John Donne|title=w:Devotions upon Emergent Occasions|location=London|publisher=Thomas Jones|pages=3–4|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A20631.0001.001
|passage={{...}} wee beggard our selues by hearkning after false riches, and '''infatuated''' our selues by hearkning after false knowledge.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1718|author=w:Daniel Defoe|title=A Continuation of Letters Written by a Turkish Spy at Paris|location=London|publisher=W. Taylor|section=Letter 4, p. 20|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004774099.0001.000
|passage=Heaven doubtless has '''infatuated''' these Infidels, and given them up to dote on the grossest Absurdities; other wise they could never swallow such open and notorious Impositions {{...}}}}

